# Playbook: pan-harness

The pan-harness (`AGENTS.md`, `PAN-HARNESS.md` and `pan-harness/`) grows old and big over time. `ph-doctor` keeps it in line with the standard, correct and compact. {{who reminds and when, for example: "On the first Monday of each month a calendar reminds, and the owner writes `/ph-doctor` to the agent."}}

## How

1. Run `ph-doctor` in the agent: `/ph-doctor` in Claude Code, Antigravity, Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Gemini CLI and OpenCode, `$ph-doctor` in Codex. If the skills are not installed (they are not kept in git): `npx @jiemurat/pan-harness@latest init`. If a new version of the pan-harness package is out: `/ph-update`.
2. Do the project steps below too.
3. Do a structural change as a large task: first the list and the plan, then the owner's approval (`AGENTS.md` → `Workflow`, `playbooks/large-task.md`). A mechanical fix the rules define is done at once.

Done when: the `ph-doctor` conformance table and report are with the owner, the project steps are done, the structural proposals are in `plan.md`.

## Growth limits

The size limits are in `project/check.json` (`start_limit_bytes` — the start set, that is the four files together; `doc_limit_bytes` — any other doc), or else the standard's; history and archive files are exempt. The numbers stand in `check.json`, the script prints them on every run. A doc over 10 KB that is read on demand starts with a `## Contents` list. `decisions.md` holds only current decisions. When `feedback.md` or `lessons.md` goes over the limit, the old entries that became rules move to the archive; lessons are kept in full. On a size warning, tidy up in the same task or tell the owner; the upkeep steps are in `## How`.

